   "error_message": "P12 cutout pin diverged: Rust Simplex.cutout_from_simplex produced 7 pieces vs oracle 5 (corners 1/2 got 2 zero-length division lines instead of 1)",
   "file": "rust/crates/epic-geometry/src/simplex.rs",
   "root_cause": "Java calcDivisionLines compares directions with REFERENCE equality: currentProjectionDir == Direction.NULL is true only for the shared constant returned by Point.perpendicularDirection in the collinear branch. The port translated it as current_projection_dir == IntDirection::NULL, but IntDirection PartialEq mirrors Java .equals (collinear side + positive projection) under which distinct NULL instances are UNEQUAL \u2014 so the zero direction from perpendicular_direction never matched, the NULL early exit never fired, and the zero direction won the min-distance scan producing a second spurious zero-length division line. The final firstProjectionDir == secondProjectionDir is also object identity (turn45Degree returns a fresh IntDirection per call), not value equality.",
   "fix": "Added IntDirection::is_null() (zero-vector check = Java reference == vs the shared NULL constant; non-zero directions never turn45Degree to zero) for both NULL guards, and replaced the final equality with a winner_is_single_projection flag that models object identity (holds exactly when the winning candidate never entered the second-division scan). P12 now pins 5 pieces.",
